  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "$4\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\times 1\\,\\frac{1}{4}=$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we write $4\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{(4\\times 2)+1}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}\n$$\n\nWe also write  $1\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{(1\\times 4)+1}{4}=\\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{2}\\times \\frac{5}{4}=\\frac{9\\times 5}{2\\times 4}=\\frac{45}{8}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{45}{8}=5\\,\\text{R}5=5\\,\\frac{5}{8}\n$$",
